    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-slavs">East Slavic</a> principalities ruled by the Rurikid dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a>, on the river route from the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a>'s conversion around 988 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a>, Cyrillic writing and Byzantine art to Rus'.</li><li>Fragmented into rival principalities after 1132, it was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a>, who sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a> in 1240.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>More than a thousand letters written on birch bark, found at Novgorod since 1951, show ordinary townspeople of Rus' writing everyday notes, debts and family messages.</p></aside>
    </section>

