    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rurik"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rurik.jpg" alt="Rurik" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rurik">Rurik</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1000_Rurik.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Дар Ветер, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kievan Rus' took shape in the ninth and tenth centuries along the river routes linking the Baltic with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the Caspian. Scandinavian traders and warriors, known as Rus' or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#varangians">Varangians</a>, moved down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volkhov-river">Volkhov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dnieper">Dnieper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volga">Volga</a> to exchange <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fur-trade">furs</a> and slaves for Byzantine goods and Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, and a Rus' polity, sometimes called the Rus' Khaganate, existed in the north by the 830s. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a> in the early twelfth century, the Slavic and Finnic tribes of the north invited the Varangian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rurik">Rurik</a> to rule them in 862, and in 882 his kinsman Oleg came south from Novgorod, killed the local rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#askold-and-dir">Askold and Dir</a> and made Kiev his seat, uniting the two ends of the route from the Varangians to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greeks">Greeks</a>. The chronicle's dates for this early period are schematic and partly legendary. Oleg and his successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#igor-of-kiev">Igor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olga-of-kiev">Olga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a> extended tribute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-slavs">East Slavic</a> tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#polans-eastern">Polianians</a>, Drevlians, Severians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#krivichs">Krivichians</a>. Igor was killed by the Drevlians in 945 while collecting tribute, and his widow Olga took revenge and reorganised tribute collection. Sviatoslav I destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> around 965, removing the main power of the steppe west of the Volga.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#constantinople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantinople.jpg" alt="Constantinople" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Constantinople-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cplakidas, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The population of Rus' was overwhelmingly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-slavs">East Slavic</a> and rural, cultivating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rye">rye</a>, wheat, barley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a> in clearings of the forest zone and on the richer soils of the forest-steppe, with Finnic and Baltic peoples in the north and west. Society was divided into princes and their retinues (<em>druzhina</em>), boyars who held land and office, free townspeople and peasants, dependent labourers and slaves. The wealth of the princes came from tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fur-trade">furs</a>, honey, wax and captives, which they shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> under trade agreements such as the treaties of 911 and 944, while hoards of Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dirham">dirhams</a> show the importance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volga">Volga</a> trade with the Muslim world in the ninth and tenth centuries. Towns grew rapidly: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a>, Novgorod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chernigov">Chernigov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smolensk">Smolensk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#polotsk">Polotsk</a> and Vladimir became centres of crafts and commerce, and Kiev under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> was one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Excavations at Novgorod since 1951 have uncovered over a thousand birch-bark documents, showing that writing was used by merchants and ordinary townspeople. The conversion of 988 brought monasteries, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Kiev Monastery of the Caves</a>, church dues and a new calendar of feasts, though pagan customs persisted in the countryside for generations, and the Church became an important landowner.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-dorostolon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-dorostolon.jpg" alt="Siege of Dorostolon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-dorostolon">Siege of Dorostolon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BelagerungvonDorostolon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Henryk Siemiradzki, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armed strength of Rus' rested on the prince's <em>druzhina</em>, a mounted and well-armed retinue that also served as his council, supplemented by town militias, tribal levies and hired Scandinavian warriors. Early princes used river fleets of dugout boats to strike far afield. The chronicle records an attack by Oleg on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 907, which Byzantine sources do not confirm, but his treaty of 911 is genuine; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#igor-of-kiev">Igor</a>'s fleet was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greek-fire">Greek fire</a> in 941. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a> (r. c. 945–972) took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sarkel">Sarkel</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atil">Itil</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-bulgarian-empire">Danubian Bulgaria</a> in 968 and was defeated by the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-dorostolon">Dorostolon</a> in 971; on his way home he was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dnieper">Dnieper</a> rapids. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> built lines of earthworks and forts south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a> against the Pechenegs and sent about 6,000 warriors to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basil-ii">Basil II</a> in 988, the origin of the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> defeated the Pechenegs decisively in 1036. From 1061 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cumans">Cumans</a>, or Polovtsy, replaced them as the main steppe threat, routing the princes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-alta-river-1068">Alta River</a> in 1068, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> led joint campaigns deep into the steppe in 1103 and 1111. Feuds between princes, however, were a constant drain on military strength.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#russkaya-pravda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda.jpg" alt="Russkaya Pravda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_instance_Sinodal%60niy_of_Pravda_Ruskaya_page_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Греков Б.Д., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kievan Rus' was less a unified state than a dynastic federation ruled by the descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rurik">Rurik</a>. The grand prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a> held seniority, and his sons and kinsmen governed other towns, moving up to more important seats as their elders died. This system of rotation produced repeated conflict: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a>'s death in 1015 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sviatopolk-i-of-kiev">Sviatopolk</a> had his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boris-and-gleb">Boris and Gleb</a> killed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> won Kiev only in 1019, sharing the realm with his brother Mstislav until 1036. Yaroslav's reign saw the first written law, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a></em>, which restricted blood feud and increasingly replaced it with fines, and its later expanded versions set out rules on compensation, debt and inheritance. The Church was headed by a metropolitan of Kiev normally appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>, though Yaroslav installed the native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hilarion-of-kiev">Hilarion</a> in 1051. Towns had assemblies (<em>veche</em>) that could summon or expel princes, above all in Novgorod. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#council-of-liubech">Council of Liubech</a> in 1097 tried to end the feuds by declaring that each prince should hold his father's patrimony, which in practice confirmed the division of the land into hereditary principalities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>, invited to Kiev by its townspeople after a riot in 1113, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mstislav-i-of-kiev">Mstislav the Great</a> were the last princes to hold the federation together.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#golden-gate-kyiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-gate-kyiv.jpg" alt="Golden Gate (Kyiv)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#golden-gate-kyiv">Golden Gate (Kyiv)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Gate_Kiev_2018_G1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Chernilevsky, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kievan Rus' brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-slavs">East Slavs</a> into the orbit of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a>. Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olga-of-kiev">Olga</a> was baptised in the 950s, and around 988 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> accepted baptism, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anna-of-east-anglia">Anna</a>, sister of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basil-ii">Basil II</a>, and had the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a> baptised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dnieper">Dnieper</a>. With Orthodoxy came the Church Slavonic liturgy, writing in the Cyrillic alphabet, Byzantine architecture and icon painting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> built the Cathedral of Saint Sophia in Kiev, with its mosaics and frescoes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-gate-kyiv">Golden Gate</a>, and a native literature followed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hilarion-of-kiev">Hilarion</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sermon-on-law-and-grace">Sermon on Law and Grace</a></em>, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a></em>, the <em>Instruction</em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#the-tale-of-igors-campaign">Tale of Igor's Campaign</a></em>, which describes a failed raid against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cumans">Cumans</a> in 1185. Yaroslav's children married into the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, a sign of the dynasty's standing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>; his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anna-of-kiev">Anna of Kiev</a> became queen of France as the wife of Henry I. The conversion of Rus' fixed the religious and cultural divide between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> eastern Europe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> West for centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> all regard Rus' as the foundation of their history, and the question of its legacy, central to the national narratives of all three, remains politically charged today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-kalka-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river.jpg" alt="Battle of the Kalka River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-kalka-river">Battle of the Kalka River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MongolCavalrymen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mstislav-i-of-kiev">Mstislav the Great</a> in 1132 the unity of Rus' dissolved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyiv">Kiev</a> remained the senior city and a coveted prize, but it was contested by several branches of the dynasty and lost its primacy as other principalities grew stronger: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chernigov">Chernigov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smolensk">Smolensk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volhynia">Volhynia</a> in the south and west, Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suzdal">Suzdal</a> in the north-east, and the Novgorod Republic, which from 1136 chose and dismissed its own princes. In 1169 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> of Vladimir-Suzdal sacked Kiev and gave it to a junior relative rather than ruling there himself, a sign that the political centre had shifted. Kiev was sacked again in 1203 during wars between princes. The decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dnieper">Dnieper</a> trade route as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> weakened, especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, further reduced the city's wealth. A first Mongol force crushed an army of Rus' princes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cumans">Cumans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-kalka-river">Battle of the Kalka River</a> in 1223 but withdrew. In 1237 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>'s armies returned, destroying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ryazan">Ryazan</a> and Vladimir in 1237–1238 and Chernigov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pereiaslav">Pereyaslavl</a> in 1239, and in December 1240 they stormed Kiev. Most of the principalities became tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the lands of Rus' were later divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a> and the rising principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kievan Rus' was the first great state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-slavs">East Slavs</a>, a federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rurik-dynasty">Rurikid</a> principalities on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dnieper">Dnieper</a> trade route whose adoption of Byzantine Orthodoxy around 988 shaped the religion, writing and culture of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, and from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> all trace their origins.</em></p>

